\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless stated otherwise, results are for Bernoulli assignment and $\sigma=0.5$ on the synthetic graphs of Section~\ref{sec:design}; intervals are Monte Carlo intervals with graph draws as the unit of replication.

\subsection{Variance follows leverage; bias does not}
\label{sec:varbias}

\paragraph{Variance.}
The sampling variance of the regression estimator follows $\sigma^2L/n$. Where the specification is correct (the plain SBM at $\sigma=0.5$, the no-spillover column and mechanism (a) at $s=\tfrac12$; the 42 cells of the $n$, $d$ and $p$ slices), log SD falls with $n$ with slope $-0.50$, rises with $1+d$ with slope $+0.55$ [0.547, 0.556] and falls with $p(1-p)$ with slope $-0.50$ (predicted: $\pm\tfrac12$; Figure~\ref{fig:var}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). The $d$ slope is therefore not $\tfrac12$: the realised leverage scales as $L\propto n^{-0.02}(1+d)^{1.09}[p(1-p)]^{-0.99}$ over 210 graph draws, so its square root has exponent $0.55$ in $1+d$.

\paragraph{Bias.}

The gains must be read next to absolute held-out $R^2$ of log $|{\rm bias}|$: on the 1224 cells where the population-OLS prediction exists, every data-computable predictor has a negative absolute held-out $R^2$ ($\bar S$ $-0.22$, noise-adjusted $S$ $-0.06$, corner times curvature $-0.12$, the best degree benchmark $p(1-p)$ $-0.26$, a constant $-0.24$), while the population-OLS prediction, which needs the true response, reaches 0.987. An exploratory noise-adjusted $S$ gains 0.20 [0.10, 0.30] over a benchmark that is no better than a constant, but its confirmatory test on 666 new cells was inconclusive (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}), so it is not evidence. We conclude that these data give \emph{no evidence of a useful data-computable bias score}, which is not evidence that none exists: the intervals include both. One reading is that the bias is determined by the response at the corners, which data inside the observed exposure range do not identify; this is an interpretation, not a test.
