\paragraph{Bias.}
The population-OLS prediction of the bias (Eq.~\ref{eq:bias}; exact in form, it uses the true response and the realised graph) lies inside the Monte Carlo interval of the measured bias in 85.1\% of 1224 cells, and in 86.7\% with the finite-$n$ centring of the fit (added post hoc: for the diffusion mechanism (e), 81\% to 90\%). The median absolute bias over $p$ and $s$ varies by under 15\% across $d=5,20,80$ for the diffusion and interaction mechanisms (e), (f); it rises for the saturating mechanism (c) from 0.19 to 0.33 and for the threshold mechanism (d) from 0.17 to 0.45--0.48. The bias of the count mechanism (b) equals $s\,[\mathrm{HM}(d_i)/\mathrm{AM}(d_i)-1]$ (correlation 0.999 over 60 runs; exact for tree-like neighbourhoods, untested beyond), so it falls with $d$ where degrees are near-Poisson (SBM, ER: 0.115, 0.025, 0.007 at $d=5,20,80$ on the SBM) and not on Barab\'asi--Albert ($-0.12$ to $-0.16$), Stanford3 ($-0.26$ to $-0.44$) or ego-Facebook ($-0.27$ to $-0.41$). For the saturating mechanism the bias changes sign at $p=0.397$ [0.393, 0.401] at $d=20$ on a grid of step 0.05, where the population-OLS prediction, which uses the true response, is 0.397, as the curvature term predicts; this tests the extrapolation account and does not locate the bias-free $p$ for a practitioner (Figure~\ref{fig:pdep}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}: $d=5$, 80). Figure~\ref{fig:bias} shows bias and SD separately.

\subsection{Where regression wins and where it loses}
\label{sec:regime}

The regression has smaller RMSE than DM whenever the spillover share is large enough for DM's bias $-s\gate$ to exceed the regression's error (Figure~\ref{fig:regime}; ``wins'' is the paired interval below zero). Over the grid of this study (Table~\ref{tab:regime}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}) regression wins in 79.6\%, 77.8\%, 69.9\%, 55.8\% and 31.3\% of cells at $\sigma=0.25,0.5,1,2,4$ ($\sigma=2,4$ are post-freeze extensions). At $\sigma=0.5$ it is 0.49 for the threshold mechanism (d) and 0.75--0.87 for the others. The break-even spillover share $s^*$ at $d=20$ is about 0.19 for (a), (b), (c), (e), 0.25 for (f), and does not exist for (d), where regression loses at every share on every synthetic graph.

The failure on the threshold mechanism does not transfer to the thinned real graphs: regression wins on (d) at all three degrees (log ratio $-1.19$ to $-0.33$ on ego-Facebook, $-1.15$ to $-0.89$ on Stanford3) and in every other real-graph row except (b) at $d=80$ on ego-Facebook, where it wins in two of three shares. The cause is not diagnosed. At $d\approx20$, $p=\tfrac12$ the real graphs are not nearer the threshold (share of exposures within $\pm0.15$ of it 0.56--0.57, synthetic 0.64--0.68) but more spread (SD 0.20--0.21, synthetic 0.11--0.13), with about a third of the bias (0.20--0.25, synthetic 0.68--0.77). A degree tail alone (Barab\'asi--Albert), clustering alone (Watts--Strogatz) or both (Holme--Kim) does not reproduce it, and log-normal degrees come part of the way at $d=20$ only; a wide exposure spread goes with the smaller bias at $d=20$ but not at $d=80$ (exploratory; Appendix~\ref{app:extra}).
